## 4. The first blocking theorem

The Clay chain, bottom-up, is:

```
constructive integration theory
   → normalized invariant (Haar) integral on SU(N)                  [L1 input]
   → finite-volume lattice Yang–Mills measure                       [L1]
   → Balaban RG estimates / small-field survival                    [L2–L4]
   → infinite-volume and continuum limits                           [L5–L6]
   → OS axioms, reflection positivity, Wightman reconstruction      [L7]
   → physical Hamiltonian, no spectral pollution, positive gap      [L8]
   → exact Clay-statement equivalence for compact simple SU(N)      [L9]
```

The first missing item is not any of L2–L9. It is at the very bottom:

> **Obstruction.** There is no proved inhabitant of `NormalizedHaarTheory` for
> any nontrivial compact group in this repository, and there cannot be one from
> the material present, because *no integration theory of any kind exists* —
> not in `DASHI`, not in `vendor/bishop`, and not in the Agda standard library.
> A repository-wide search for integration (`∫`, `integral`, `measure`,
> `Riemann`) finds only: the postulated `NormalizedHaarTheory` field
> (`CompactLieAnalyticPackage.agda:118`), abstract Riemann-cell algebra in
> `MarxFiniteRiemannSumLaws.agda`, and rational bookkeeping constants such as
> `configuredHaarUpper` in
> `BalabanClayT2ConfiguredComponentLossBoundsExact.agda:45`, which are numeric
> budgets, not measures.

Why this blocks everything above it. The finite-volume lattice Yang–Mills
measure of L1 *is* a product of normalized Haar measures over the links, and
every observable, partition function, correlation, transfer-matrix and
Osterwalder–Schrader statement above L1 is phrased as an integral against it.
With `haar` supplied by `standardAnalysisForCompactSimple`, every statement in
the tower is conditional on a postulate; deleting that postulate removes the
lattice measure and with it L1–L9. This is exactly the situation forbidden by
the task's rules (no closure through an imported authority), so the honest
report is: the chain is blocked at its base.

Smallest precise statement that must be proved first, and is not:

> Let `SU2` be the unit-quaternion group. There exists `I : (SU2 → ℝ) → ℝ`
> defined on all uniformly continuous `f`, with `I (λ _ → 1) = 1`, `I` linear
> and monotone, and `I (λ x → f (g · x)) = I f = I (λ x → f (x · g))` for all
> `g : SU2` — i.e. an inhabitant of `NormalizedHaarTheory` for `SU2` over a
> *constructed* real carrier.

## 5. New machine-checked content added in this session

Four modules were added. All are `--without-K --safe`, all typecheck, and none
contains a `postulate`, an opaque `Set` field, a receipt or an appeal to
authority. They work over the vendored Bishop reals, i.e. over a *constructed*
carrier, not over `RealAnalysisAxioms`.

- `DASHI/Analysis/BishopFiniteSumLemmas.agda` — the finite-sum calculus needed
  for Riemann sums: `natToℝ` and `natToℝ-⋆`, `∑₀-congruent`, `∑₀-shift-split`,
  `∑₀-block` (an `n·p`-term sum equals `n` blocks of `p`), `∑₀-abs` (triangle
  inequality), `∑₀-const`, `∑₀-scale`, `∑₀-sub`, `∑₀-const-bound` and
  `∑₀-const-bound-lt`.

- `DASHI/Analysis/BishopRiemannIntegral.agda` — a constructive Riemann integral
  on `[0,1]`:
  - `width`, `node`, `riemannSum f m` (uniform partition into `suc m` cells,
    left endpoints), `refine m q` (each cell cut into `suc q` pieces, with
    `suc (refine m q) ≡ suc m * suc q` *definitionally*), `comm-refine`;
  - `width-refine`, `width-unit` (`(1/(m+1)) · (m+1) = 1`, i.e. the cells of a
    partition of `[0,1]` add to 1), `node-refine`, `node-close`;
  - `UniformModulus f`: a modulus of uniform continuity indexed by naturals;
  - `refinement-bound`: refining a partition finer than the modulus changes the
    Riemann sum by at most `1/(j+1)`;
  - `riemannSum-isCauchy`: the Riemann sums form a Cauchy sequence (via the
    common refinement `refine m₁ m₂`);
  - `integral` and `riemannSum-converges`: the integral is *defined* as the
    limit given by Bishop's `cauchy⇒convergent`, together with the theorem that
    the uniform Riemann sums converge to it;
  - `integral-const` and `integral-one`: the integral of a constant is that
    constant, in particular the normalisation `∫₀¹ 1 = 1`, so the construction
    is not degenerate.

- `DASHI/Analysis/BishopRiemannIntegralAdditivity.agda` — `∑₀-add`,
  `riemannSum-add`, a modulus `sumModulus` for a sum of two uniformly
  continuous functions, and `integral-add`: the integral is additive.

- `DASHI/Analysis/BishopPeriodicShift.agda` and
  `DASHI/Analysis/BishopCircleInvariance.agda` — the translation theory:
  - `node-add`, `node-period`, and `uc-cong` (a function with a modulus of
    uniform continuity respects equality of Bishop reals — needed because
    `ℝ → ℝ` functions are not automatically setoid morphisms);
  - `periodicShiftSum` and `riemannSum-shift`: for a 1-periodic uniformly
    continuous `f`, the uniform Riemann sum on `[0,1]` is *exactly* invariant
    under a shift by any multiple of the mesh;
  - `node-scale`, `refine-mono`, `shiftModulus`, `rationalShiftSum`;
  - `integral-rationalShift`: **the integral of a 1-periodic uniformly
    continuous function is invariant under translation by every rational point
    `t/(p+1)` of the circle**, proved by passing to the subsequence of
    partitions refining `p` and using uniqueness of limits.

So the added material is a normalised, additive, rational-translation-invariant
integral on the circle group `ℝ/ℤ` — a constructed (not postulated) object of
exactly the shape that `NormalizedHaarTheory` specifies, for the simplest
nontrivial compact group, minus invariance under arbitrary real translations.

## 6. Distance from the new brick to the Clay statement

What is still missing before section 4's obstruction is discharged:

1. invariance under *arbitrary* real translations of the circle. The rational
   case is proved; the general case needs (i) invariance under integer shifts,
   (ii) rational approximation of an arbitrary real shift, and (iii) a
   comparison lemma turning a uniform pointwise bound `|g x − h x| ≤ ε` into
   `|∫g − ∫h| ≤ ε`. None of these is written.
2. monotonicity and scalar homogeneity of the integral (routine, unwritten).
3. iterated integration on `[0,1]³` with a Fubini-type interchange for
   uniformly continuous integrands.
4. an explicit uniformly-continuous chart of SU(2) by `[0,1]³` (Euler-angle or
   quaternion polar coordinates) *over the Bishop reals*, together with its
   Jacobian weight — note that the existing quaternion carrier
   (`BalabanSU2QuaternionCarrier.agda`) is built over the postulated reals and
   would have to be rebuilt over the constructed ones;
5. left and right invariance of the resulting weighted integral on SU(2) —
   which is where the circle argument above stops generalising, because the
   group is no longer abelian and the invariance is no longer a cyclic
   permutation of a one-dimensional sum;
6. the same for SU(N), N ≥ 3, where the standard route (Weyl integration
   formula) needs root-system and maximal-torus theory that the repository does
   not have.

Only after (1)–(6) does L1 (the finite-volume lattice measure) become
constructible; L2–L9 — Balaban's renormalization-group estimates with
small-field survival, the infinite-volume and continuum limits, OS positivity
and Wightman reconstruction, convergence of the physical Hamiltonian without
spectral pollution, and a positive continuum mass gap — remain open research
mathematics, not merely unformalized. The repository's own `Round11CompleteCutset`
already encodes this by keeping `fixedLatticeDLRLSIGapChain` and
`continuumOSAndSIMassGapChain` as hypotheses at `conditional` level.

