## A.2 What this is *not*

- It is not a Yang-Mills result. U(1) is abelian; compact *simple* SU(N) is
  what the Clay statement requires, and the whole difficulty of that statement
  — asymptotic freedom, the Bałaban renormalization-group estimates, the
  continuum limit, the positive mass gap — is invisible in the abelian case.
- It does not inhabit the repository's own `NormalizedHaarTheory`
  (`CompactLieAnalyticPackage.agda`, lines 113-134). That record is stated over
  the postulated reals of `DASHI/Foundations/RealAnalysisAxioms.agda` and uses
  propositional equality `_≡_` of reals in `normalized`, `leftInvariant` and
  `rightInvariant`. Over any constructed real carrier — Bishop's included —
  equality of reals is a setoid relation `_≃_`, not `_≡_`, so that record as
  written cannot be inhabited by a construction; it can only be postulated.
  This is a defect of the surface, not of the mathematics: the constructive
  statements proved here are the `_≃_` versions of exactly those fields.
- Nothing above bears on rows A-D of the frozen research count. Those rows are
  about the Bałaban trajectory, marked-source locality, the Heat/Doob mass gap
  and the short-distance OPE, and none of them is touched.

## A.3 The remaining route to SU(N), stated precisely

Items 4-6 of section 6 are unchanged, and the honest shape of the remaining
work is now clearer, because the tools built here are the ones that route
needs. Two candidate routes:

**Route I (charts and change of variables).** SU(2) is the unit 3-sphere in ℝ⁴.
Constructing its Haar integral this way needs, in order: (i) the Riemann
integral over an arbitrary box in ℝⁿ, obtained from the present cube integral
by an affine substitution; (ii) translation invariance in ℝⁿ for compactly
supported uniformly continuous integrands; (iii) invariance under shear
(transvection) maps — this is exactly where `fubini` is used, since a shear
moves one coordinate only; (iv) invariance under SL(4,ℝ), by decomposing an
orthogonal matrix into transvections; (v) the radial construction
`∫_{S³} f dσ ∝ ∫_{annulus} f(x/∣x∣) dx`, which needs a constructive square
root and a cutoff away from the origin to keep the integrand uniformly
continuous; (vi) the observation that left multiplication by a unit quaternion
is an orthogonal map of ℝ⁴, which then gives left and right invariance at once.
None of (i)-(vi) is written.

**Route II (averaging over the group).** For a compact metric group one can
build the Haar mean of a uniformly continuous `f` as the limit of averages of
translates over finite ε-nets, the classical von Neumann argument, with the
oscillation of the averages contracting geometrically in the size of the net.
This route needs SU(2) as a *constructive* compact metric group over the Bishop
reals — in particular explicit ε-nets with proved covering estimates — which
the repository does not have either; `BalabanSU2QuaternionCarrier.agda` is
built over the postulated reals.

Either way the group-theoretic input for SU(N) is still missing, so the
verdict of section 0 stands unchanged: **the Jaffe-Witten theorem is not
closed in this repository, and nothing here claims otherwise.**

# Addendum B, 2026-08-21 (later): unimodularity, shears, and factorization

Four further postulate-free, `--safe` results were added over the constructed
Bishop reals. They extend the invariance group of the constructed integral
beyond translations, which is the part of Route I (item (iii) above) that could
be settled with the tools already present.

1. `DASHI/Analysis/BishopTorusLinearity.agda` — `torusIntegral-product`: an
   integrand of the form `g(v₀)·h(v₁,…,v_{n-1})` has factorizing integral. For
   a lattice measure this is independence of distinct links, i.e. the product
   structure of the measure, proved rather than assumed.
2. `DASHI/Analysis/BishopTorusShear.agda` — `shear₁-invariant` and
   `shear₂-invariant`: the two-dimensional integral is invariant under the
   transvections `(x,y) ↦ (x+y, y)` and `(x,y) ↦ (x, y+x)`. The first uses only
   the one-dimensional real-translation invariance applied with the outer
   variable frozen; the second additionally uses `fubini`, since there the
   translated variable is the outer one. These are the first genuinely
   non-translational changes of variables in the tree, and they are exactly the
   generators of `SL(2,ℤ)` acting on the 2-torus.
3. `DASHI/Analysis/BishopCircleReflection.agda` — `integral-reflect`:
   `∫ f(−x) = ∫ f(x)` for 1-periodic uniformly continuous `f`, i.e. the
   constructed circle measure is invariant under the group inversion
   (unimodularity). The proof is exact finite combinatorics: reflected nodes
   are the original nodes traversed backwards modulo the period, so the two
   Riemann sums agree *identically* at every partition, and the two integrals
   are then squeezed together by the explicit rate of `BishopIntegralRate`.
   Supporting general lemmas proved on the way: bounded congruence of finite
   sums, reversal of the summation order (`∑₀-rev`), and the complementary-node
   identity `node m (N − i) ≃ 1 − node m i`.
4. `DASHI/Analysis/BishopTorusReflection.agda` — `torus-reflect-canonical`:
   the same statement for the n-torus, lifted coordinate by coordinate; and, in
   `DASHI/Physics/YangMills/BishopU1LatticeHaarMeasure.agda`, the new field
   `orientInvar` of `LatticeGaugeMeasure`, proved for the U(1) inhabitant:
   reversing the orientation of every link leaves every expectation unchanged.

